Roman to Integer
Approach

Roman numerals are generally written from largest to smallest. However, there are six special cases where a smaller numeral appears before a larger one to indicate subtraction, such as IV (4) and IX (9).

Algorithm
We use a HashMap (Map<Character, Integer>) to store the integer value corresponding to each Roman numeral.
Traverse the string from left to right.
For each character:
Retrieve the value of the current numeral (curr).
Retrieve the value of the next numeral (next), if it exists.
If curr < next, the current value should be subtracted from the result.
Otherwise, the current value is added to the result.
int curr = map.get(s.charAt(i));
int next = (i + 1 < s.length()) ? map.get(s.charAt(i + 1)) : 0;

if (curr < next) {
    res -= curr;
} else {
    res += curr;
}

By checking only the current and next symbols, we naturally handle all subtractive cases (IV, IX, XL, XC, CD, and CM) without requiring additional conditions.

Complexity
Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)